"At fifteen he read Bentham, and the principle of utility, he said, gave unity to everything he knew: he now had a creed, a philosophy, a religion. At seventeen he was briefly arrested for handing out pamphlets on birth control in the poor streets of London, and in the same year he followed his father into the London office of the East India Company, where he would work for thirty-five years, drafting dispatches to India in the mornings and writing philosophy in the time left over.": "6bbfb14aad91",
"Bentham had held that pleasure is the only good, that pleasures differ only in amount, and that pushpin is as good as poetry if it pleases as much. Critics called this a philosophy fit for swine. Mill kept the foundation: actions are right in proportion as they tend to promote happiness, wrong as they tend to produce the reverse of happiness, and the happiness that counts is that of everyone affected, extended so far as the nature of things admits, to the whole sentient creation. What he changed was the account of happiness. Pleasures differ in kind as well as in amount, he argued, and the test is the verdict of those who have known both. No one who has enjoyed the exercise of intelligence, feeling, and conscience would give them up for any quantity of an animals contentment. It is better to be a human being dissatisfied than a pig satisfied; better to be Socrates dissatisfied than a fool satisfied. And if the fool, or the pig, is of a different opinion, it is because they only know their own side of the question.": "65a6a323c5eb",
"Critics have never been satisfied. If the rules matter only for their results, then in the rare case where breaking one would certainly do more good and never be discovered, the theory has no reason left to keep it, and those are the cases that test a theory. A second objection concerns the higher pleasures. If some pleasures are better in kind, apart from how pleasant they are, then something other than pleasure is making them better, and Mill has quietly given up the view that pleasure is the only good. A third fastens on his proof. The sole evidence it is possible to produce that anything is desirable, he wrote, is that people do actually desire it. But visible means can be seen, and desirable means ought to be desired, and from the fact that people want something it does not follow that they should.": "64483e65fcc9",
"If right action is whatever produces the most happiness on the whole, then it seems that an innocent person may be sacrificed whenever enough others would gain. A sheriff could hang a man he knows to be innocent in order to prevent a riot in which many would die. A surgeon could take one patients organs to save five. Mills own principle of liberty appears to be in danger from his principle of utility, since a majority might be made happier by silencing a hated minority. He saw the problem and gave the last and longest chapter of Utilitarianism to it. Justice, he argues, is the name for those rules that protect the most vital of human interests, security above all, and a society that kept them only when convenient would lose the security that makes every other good possible. Rights are to be respected almost without exception because that policy has the best results.": "4846ec4cad11",
"In 1830 he met Harriet Taylor, who was twenty-two, married, and the mother of two children. For twenty years they maintained an intense friendship that scandalized London and that her husband tolerated. Two years after his death they married, in 1851, and Mill signed a formal protest against the legal powers that marriage gave him over her. She died at Avignon in 1858. He bought a house within sight of the cemetery and spent half of every year there. On Liberty appeared the following year, dedicated to her as the inspirer, and in part the author, of all that is best in my writings.": "31cf3d1c4dc0",
"In 1865 the voters of Westminster elected him to Parliament, though he had refused to campaign or to spend any money. In 1867 he moved that the word man in the Reform Bill be replaced by person, which would have given women the vote. The motion was lost, and seventy-three members voted for it, far more than anyone expected. The Subjection of Women followed in 1869. He died at Avignon in 1873 and was buried beside Harriet. He had agreed, the year before, to stand as godfather to the infant son of a friend. The child was Bertrand Russell.": "edbdddd32f38",
"In the autumn of 1826, when he was twenty, the machine stopped. It occurred to me to put the question directly to myself: Suppose that all your objects in life were realized; that all the changes in institutions and opinions which you are looking forward to, could be completely effected at this very instant: would this be a great joy and happiness to you? And an irrepressible self-consciousness distinctly answered, No! At this my heart sank within me: the whole foundation on which my life was constructed fell down. He had been trained to analyze and had never been taught to feel, and he now feared that the habit of analysis had worn his feelings away. For months he went through his work mechanically and told no one.": "6c6c04189907",
"James Mill, a Scottish journalist and a disciple of the reformer Jeremy Bentham, believed that the mind at birth is a blank and that education can make of it whatever it chooses. He decided to prove it on his eldest son. I have no remembrance of the time when I began to learn Greek, John Stuart Mill wrote. I have been told that it was when I was three years old. Latin followed at eight, with geometry and algebra. By twelve he had read most of the Greek and Latin classics and was set to logic, and at thirteen he went through a complete course of political economy on daily walks with his father, writing up each lesson the next morning. He was kept from other children. He was not allowed holidays, lest the habit of work be broken. He reckoned afterward that the method had given him an advantage of a quarter of a century over his contemporaries.": "3b4a1ddb8e4b",
"Mills life is known chiefly from his Autobiography, written in the 1850s and revised until his death. It is a remarkable document and a partial one. It is organized as the history of an education. His mother is not mentioned in it, and his eight younger brothers and sisters, most of whom he taught, hardly appear. Of Harriet Taylor he writes in terms that readers have always found extravagant. He credits her with a mind greater than his own, and says of their later books that the most valuable ideas and features in these joint productions originated with her. Scholars have disagreed ever since about how literally to take him. Little survives in her hand beyond an essay on the enfranchisement of women and her letters. Some hold that he exaggerated out of love and grief. Others point out that the works written during and just after their marriage, On Liberty above all, differ in spirit from what he wrote before, and that the assumption that a woman could not have contributed much is the very prejudice the two of them wrote against.": "51138045b0a1",
"Mills morality and the Buddhas have the same scope. Both take the suffering and happiness of every sentient being to count, both refuse to give ones own a special weight, and Mills phrase the whole sentient creation could stand as a translation of the Buddhist wish that all beings be happy. Mills crisis also taught him something the Buddha had taught. Happiness sought directly recedes. Mills remedy was to aim at some other end and find happiness by the way. The Buddhist analysis goes a step further and says why: the seeking is itself the craving that makes contentment impossible.": "fb377ce66c31",
"On Liberty applies the same standard to society. A people is happiest in the long run, Mill argues, when its members are free to form their own opinions and try their own ways of living, because truth is found only by the collision of opinions and character is formed only by choice. He proposes one principle to mark the limit of what a society may force on a person: the only purpose for which power can rightfully be exercised over anyone against his will is to prevent harm to others. A persons own good is not a sufficient warrant. And silencing an opinion is always a loss: If all mankind minus one, were of one opinion, and only one person were of the contrary opinion, mankind would be no more justified in silencing that one person, than he, if he had the power, would be justified in silencing mankind.": "ba3971077b1b",
"The first relief came by accident. He was reading the memoirs of the French writer Marmontel and came to the passage describing the death of the authors father, and he found himself in tears. He was not, then, a stock or a stone. Two years later he read Wordsworth, whose poems gave him what he called a culture of the feelings. He drew two lessons that shaped everything he later wrote. Happiness cannot be had by aiming at it: Ask yourself whether you are happy, and you cease to be so. And a good life needs the cultivation of the inner person as well as the reform of outward circumstances. He never renounced Benthams principle. He spent the rest of his life enlarging what it meant.": "133941a93855",
"They differ on three points. Mill judges an act by its results and holds that the motive has nothing to do with the morality of the action. The Buddha judges it by the intention, and a harmful result that was not intended is not an unwholesome deed. Second, Mill adds happiness across persons, so that a loss to one can be outweighed by gains to many. Buddhist ethics has no such sum. It asks each agent not to harm, and the bodhisattva who gives his body to feed a tigress gives his own and no one elses. Third, they mean different things by happiness. Mills higher pleasures are the satisfactions of a cultivated mind, and they remain satisfactions of desire. The Buddha ranks above all of these a happiness that comes from the ending of desire. Whether that is a still higher pleasure on Mills scale or something the scale cannot measure is a good question to put to both (Dharma and the West, D18).": "21e275bc811f",
